(dest/'README.md').write_text('''# Textured colour echoes — native Studio

Watch **Textured colour echoes.mp4**. Cyan, magenta and orange copies carry the dancers' actual moving face and clothing pixels. This replaces the flat-fill approach. The natural-colour lead remains in front. Original music and dancing speed are retained; no added line graphics are used.

Reusable feature: Studio → Motion & Flow → Node Flow → Subject echoes & light arcs → Tinted photographic echoes. Per-copy colour, strength, midtone gamma and shadow lift are under Composite → Photographic tint. Light arcs default off. The native project retains8 ordinary video layers and36 keys. Controls, individual colour edit, Undo/Redo, independent restore and exact native CPU export binding are recorded.

The matching subject matte is the previously prepared source-bound CPU analysis input. This feature does not automatically isolate people, physically relight a scene or generate the reference image's energy ribbons. Multi-person matte edges remain imperfect. This is an8-second/192-frame/24fps/576×1024 study of original source8–16s, not a full-length dance edit or an exact recreation of the reference picture.

The actual Studio authoring, photographic control/refinement recordings and full live/finished timeline playback accompany the separate final movie and editable native backup. Captures are muted; their observed update rates are in Technical/verification.json. Small grey-step colour checks differ by at most1/255 per channel, but whole picture/alpha-edge/sound parity and human listening remain unqualified. This is a local implementation; no production deployment, paid provider/GPU execution, publishing or scheduling occurred. Earlier files are preserved. Keep the supplied source/matte for native relinking if the isolated local media library is unavailable.
''')
